  "articleBody": "It's frustrating when you know your family law firm has the answers, but AI systems like Google AI Overviews or ChatGPT aren't citing you. This usually boils down to how AI systems find and understand information. Unlike traditional SEO that targets keywords, AI visibility (or AEO) needs content that directly answers questions in a structured, machine-readable way.\n\nMany family law practices have great content, but it might be buried in heavy website themes or lack the specific structured data (like JSON-LD schema) that AI systems rely on. If your FAQs are thin, outdated, or not explicitly designed for machine consumption, you're likely missing out. AI bots don't just read; they parse and prioritize content that's clear, concise, and structured specifically for their algorithms.\n\nYour existing website might not be speaking the AI's language. Heavy CMS platforms often slow down crawlers, preventing AI from efficiently accessing your valuable content. Plus, if you're not systematically targeting the *real* questions people are asking on platforms like Avvo or Reddit, your firm won't be seen as the go-to source.\n\nWhat to Prepare Before You Start\nBefore diving in, think about the specific questions your family law clients frequently ask during consultations or on intake forms. These are the goldmines. Also, consider any high-risk subjects like emergency custody or protective orders where accuracy and up-to-date information are critical. Having an attorney review these substantive explanations and jurisdictional distinctions is super important, ensuring everything is ethically sound and legally accurate.\n\nGetting this right means transitioning from simply having information online to having information that's *answer-ready* for AI. It's about engineering your content so AI systems trust and surface your expertise.",
